/// The router component's world.
///
/// A router is a stepped device (`wasmnet:link/stepped-device`: `ports`, `receive`, `poll`,
/// `link-state`; config through `wasi:config/store`; events through `wasmnet:link/log`) that also
/// exports `wasmnet:link/counters`, so tests can read its per-port and forwarding counters after a
/// run, and `wasmnet:control/commands`, so its static routes, port forwards, allow rules and
/// tunnels can change while it runs. It needs no clock and no randomness: time arrives as `now`
/// arguments, and its next deadline (an ARP/NDP retry, the earliest expiry of a translation
/// session or a filter flow, or a tunnel's next check) is `poll`'s answer.
///
/// # Commands
///
/// `wasmnet:control/commands.invoke(now, command, argument)` takes these commands. A route is
/// written in the configuration's own syntax (`routes`: `10.0.3.0/24 via 10.0.2.2`,
/// `default via 10.0.2.2`, `fd00:3::/64 via fe80::2 port 1`, `10.9.0.0/16 port 1`), checked
/// against the router's ports exactly as at start. The table holds one route per prefix.
///
/// - `route-add <route>`: adds a static route. `ok("added")`; `ok("unchanged")` when that exact
///   route is there already; an error when another route holds its prefix (use
///   `route-replace`).
/// - `route-replace <route>`: adds the route, or replaces the static route for its prefix.
///   `ok("added")`, `ok("replaced")` or `ok("unchanged")`.
/// - `route-remove <prefix>` or `route-remove <route>`: removes the static route for the
///   prefix (`0.0.0.0/0` or `::/0` for a default route); given a whole route, only if it is
///   that route. `ok("removed")`; `ok("absent")` when there is none.
/// - `routes` (no argument): the table, one route per line in table order (connected routes
///   first, then static routes in the order they were added; a replaced route keeps its place),
///   as `<prefix> [via <next-hop>] port <n> (connected|static)`.
///
/// Every mutation is idempotent: asking for what is already true succeeds and changes nothing.
/// A connected route (a port's own prefix) can be neither added, replaced nor removed, and an
/// unknown command, a malformed route or a port that does not exist is an `err` that changes
/// nothing.
///
/// What a change touches, and what it does not:
///
/// - It applies at `now`, to every lookup from then on: the next packet forwarded or
///   originated takes the new route. A `poll(now)` follows the call, as after every command.
/// - Packets already waiting for a neighbour's ARP or NDP answer stay queued for that neighbour
///   and leave when it answers (or draw a host unreachable when it never does), as in a kernel,
///   where the neighbour queue comes after routing.
/// - The neighbour cache, the ports and their addresses, the links (their names, carriers and
///   captures) and the counters are untouched: a new next hop that is already known is used at
///   once, without an ARP request or Neighbor Solicitation.
/// - Each change is journalled: `route-added {prefix, via, port}` and `route-removed {prefix,
///   via, port}` (`via` is `on-link` for a route without a next hop); a replacement is a
///   `route-removed` then a `route-added`. A change also resets the once-per-destination
///   `route-lookup` trace (`trace.route-lookup = first`), so the next lookup of each destination
///   is reported with its new route.
/// - Counters: `routes` is the table's size; `routes-added` and `routes-removed` count the
///   changes (a replacement counts in both) and are listed once a command has changed the
///   table, so a router whose routes never change keeps its counter list.
///
/// # Port forwards and allow rules
///
/// Since 0.1.2. On a router with a translating port (`port<N>.nat = masquerade`), port forwards
/// change through these commands; on any router, the allow rules of the packet filter
/// (`docs/design/nat.md`). Both are idempotent like the route commands, apply to packets from
/// `now` on, and are journalled.
///
/// - `forward-add <forward>`: a forward in the configuration's syntax (`nat.forwards`):
///   `<tcp|udp> <external-port> to <addr>:<port> [port <n>]`, where `port <n>` names the
///   translating router port (the lowest one by default). `ok("added")`, `ok("unchanged")`
///   when that forward exists; an error when its external port is forwarded already or held by
///   a live mapping (until it expires), or when the inside endpoint has a forward on that port.
///   Journalled as `nat-forward-added {proto, external, internal, port}`.
/// - `forward-remove <forward>` or `forward-remove <proto> <external-port> [port <n>]`: removes
///   the forward and the sessions through it (given a whole forward, only if it is that one).
///   `ok("removed")` or `ok("absent")`; journalled as `nat-forward-removed`.
/// - `forwards` (no argument): one forward per line, `<proto> <external> to <addr>:<port> port
///   <n>`, in the order they were added.
/// - `mappings` (no argument): the translation table, one mapping per line, `<proto>
///   <internal> -> <external> port <n> (remote <any|addr|addr:port>|forward)`, each followed by
///   its sessions, `  session <remote>[ <tcp state>]`.
/// - `allow-add <port> <rule>`: appends a rule to router port `<port>`'s allow list. A rule is
///   `<any|tcp|udp|icmp|icmpv6> [from <prefix>] [to <prefix>] [dport <p>|<lo>-<hi>]`, as in
///   `port<N>.allow`. `ok("added")` or `ok("unchanged")`. A port that gets its first rule
///   filters from then on; flows already crossing it are not known to it. Journalled as
///   `allow-added {port, rule}`.
/// - `allow-remove <port> <rule>`: removes that rule. `ok("removed")` or `ok("absent")`;
///   journalled as `allow-removed`.
/// - `rules` (no argument): every rule, one per line, `<port> <rule>`, by port.
///
/// On a router without a translating port the forward commands and `mappings` are an `err`.
/// The `nat-*` counters are listed only by a router with a translating port, the `filter-*`
/// counters only by one that has, or had, an allow rule.
///
/// # Tunnels
///
/// Since 0.1.3. A tunnel is a point-to-point router port without a cable: a packet routed to it
/// leaves in Geneve over UDP from the router's own address toward the tunnel's peer, and STUN
/// Binding checks on the same UDP port find the peer through NATs, keep its mapping open and
/// follow it (`docs/design/tunnels.md`). A tunnel takes the settings of the config's
/// `tunnel.<name>.<key>`, written as `<key> <value>` pairs: `id` (the VNI, 1..=16777215,
/// unique per UDP port) and `local` (the transit address, `fd00:ff::a/128`) are required;
/// `udp` (6081), `peer` and `reflector` (IPv4 endpoints, comma-separated, at most 8), `mtu`
/// (1464), `probe` (100ms), `rounds` (20), `keepalive` (25s), `dead` (80s) and `mss-clamp`
/// (true) are optional. A tunnel name is `a-z`, `0-9` and `-`, 1 to 32 characters, starting with
/// a letter. A router has at most 256 tunnels.
///
/// - `tunnel-add <name> <key> <value>...`: adds the tunnel, its port and the connected route of
///   its transit prefix; with candidates or reflectors its first checks leave at `now`.
///   `ok("added")`; `ok("unchanged")` when that tunnel exists with exactly these settings; an
///   error when it exists with others (remove it first), when its id is taken on its UDP port,
///   or at the limit.
/// - `tunnel-remove <name>`: removes the tunnel, its port, the connected route of its transit
///   prefix and every static route over it (each static one journalled as `route-removed`).
///   `ok("removed")` or `ok("absent")`. Other tunnels keep their ports and routes.
/// - `tunnel-peer <name> <ip:port>`: one more candidate endpoint of the peer, what a
///   controller learnt from the other side (its reflexive endpoint). A tunnel that is idle or
///   failed probes again at once. `ok("added")` or `ok("unchanged")`.
/// - `tunnel-peer-remove <name> <ip:port>`: forgets a candidate (a tunnel that is up keeps its
///   peer). `ok("removed")` or `ok("absent")`.
/// - `tunnels` (no argument): one tunnel per line, by name. Each line is the name, then
///   `<key> <value>` pairs in this order: `id`, `udp`, `local`, `state` (`idle`, `probing`,
///   `up` or `failed`), `peer`, `reflexive` (endpoints, `-` when unknown), `candidates`
///   (comma-separated, `-` when none), `tx-packets`, `rx-packets`. Later versions may append
///   pairs at the end of a line; they never reorder or rename these.
///
/// Routes name a tunnel instead of a port: `<prefix> tunnel <name>`, with neither `via` nor
/// `port` (`fd00:b::/64 tunnel to-b`), in the config's `routes` and in the route commands;
/// `routes` lists them so. Events and counters name tunnels too: a port field holds a tunnel's
/// name for a tunnel's port, and the counters are `tunnel-<name>-*`. The tunnel commands are
/// idempotent like the others and apply at `now`; a `poll(now)` follows each, which sends what
/// the change asks for (a check to a new candidate).
///
/// This package names that combination; it adds no interface of its own. (A world added to
/// `wasmnet:link` would change that package for every device.) 0.1.2 documented the forward and
/// allow commands and 0.1.3 the tunnel commands; nothing in the WIT changed, so a host built
/// against 0.1.1 runs it as is.
package wasmnet:router@0.1.3;

world router {
    include wasmnet:link/stepped-device@0.1.0;
    export wasmnet:link/counters@0.1.0;
    export wasmnet:control/commands@0.1.0;
}
